Chapter: DeclaredVarBodyDecides

 A type variable in a signature is universally quantified: the caller
 chooses it, so the body must answer every choice. This body answers one.

 The signature of h promises to turn any a into any b. No total function
 can honour that, and until this was caught the compiler agreed anyway:
 the body decided b := List a, the caller went on choosing b for itself,
 and nothing reconciled the two. A caller writing let n = h 5 in n + 1
 picked b := Integer and printed 6291465 -- 0x600009 against a heap base
 of 0x600000, a raw list pointer wearing the type Integer. Type confusion
 with no cast and no diagnostic, which is the one thing the type system
 exists to make unrepresentable.

 In h the variable b is the one that matters: it appears in no parameter,
 so it is free at both ends and the body decides it in the RETURN.

 The rule is not about return position, and f is here to say so. Its
 signature promises to accept any a and answer an Integer, but the body
 adds one to a, which only an Integer can do. The body decides a through a
 PARAMETER, the promise is false in the same way, and the diagnostic is the
 same. Both defs raise CDX2087; the sidecar names the code once.

  h : a -> b
  h (x) = [x]

  f : a -> Integer
  f (x) = x + 1

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
   print-line (show (f 5))
  end
